# RAGEQUIT.EXE: How Long Can You Stay Calm?

A single-file rage-bait game for Round 2. It looks like a cute, tiny game, promises "Only 5 easy levels!", and then runs the player through 12 traps that each attack a different frustration. The UI, narrator and sounds get angrier as the player does.

Everything is in one file: `ragequit.html` (HTML, CSS and vanilla JS, no frameworks, no build step).

## Run it

1. Download `ragequit.html`.
2. Double-click it to open it in any modern browser (works offline).
3. Only the Google Fonts request needs internet. Without it, fallback fonts are used.

Hosted version: https://claude.ai/artifact/DBso4Adzs6ihRrEwXqSU4W

## Structure: exactly 2 pages

The game lives on one HTML file, split into two scrolling views. Moving between them swaps content in place, with no reload and no new URL.

| View | Contents |
|------|----------|
| Page 1 | Intro card (warning, start button, leaderboard) and levels 1-6 |
| Page 2 | Levels 7-12, then the results card at the bottom |

- Levels are stacked in a scrolling column. Locked levels are blurred, and each unlocks only when the previous one is cleared.
- After level 6, a "Next page" button appears.
- Results appear as a card at the bottom of the page you are on. They are not a separate screen.
- Cards slide in on scroll. The page scrolls to the next level automatically.

## The 12 levels

| # | Level | The trap | How to beat it |
|---|-------|----------|----------------|
| 1 | Click to Win | Button dodges the cursor 3 times, then splits into 5 fakes | Click the smallest button |
| 2 | Loading 99% | Bar hits 99% and drops back to 12% | Click the tiny "cancel" link after the 3rd reset |
| 3 | Perfect 50 | Slider jitters under the mouse near 50 | Hold within 0.6 of 50 for 1s. Keyboard arrows don't jitter |
| 4 | Password Hell | Contradicting rules appear every 3s, lying strength meter | Type `correcthorsebatterystaple`, or press Submit 3 times |
| 5 | Cookie Maze | 4 stacked banners, "Accept All" is a decoy | Click the tiny moving "x" on each banner |
| 6 | Ad Apocalypse | "Skip in 5" resets on hover, fake popups with fake X buttons | Keep the mouse off Skip, wait for "Skip ▶", click it |
| 7 | Captcha | Checkbox slides away 3 times, grid reshuffles after every click | Click 3 buses in total |
| 8 | Whack-a-Mole+ | Moles pop up fast, some say "DON'T" | Hit 6 real moles |
| 9 | Opposite Day | 4 shuffled buttons | Click "DO NOT CLICK" |
| 10 | Memory Betrayal | 3 pairs, cards reshuffle after every miss | Match all pairs |
| 11 | Fake Victory | Confetti and "YOU WIN!", then "just kidding" | Click OK |
| 12 | Fake Crash | Fake blue screen | Do nothing for 10s. Any key press or click resets the timer |

## Game systems

**Rage meter (0-100%).**
- Rises with failed clicks (+3 to +8 each), with time (about +0.15 per second), and with some level events.
- Above 20% the background shifts toward red.
- Above 40% scanlines fade in.
- Above 60% the level area shakes.
- The level area also tilts slightly as rage rises.

**Narrator.**
- A speech bubble with 5 tiers of lines (calm, annoyed, frustrated, raging, chaos).
- The tier follows the rage meter.
- It also reacts to hints, level clears and mercy.

**Fail feedback.** Every fail flashes a red outline, plays an error buzz and adds rage.

**Hints and mercy (so nobody gets stuck).**
- After 2 fails on a level, the narrator gives a hint.
- After 3 fails, or after 18 seconds on a level, a "Mercy: next level" button appears and skips the level.

**Audio.** WebAudio only (click, error buzz, trombone, fanfare). No audio files. A mute toggle is always in the top bar.

**Ticker.** A scrolling bar of fake stats at the bottom ("Player_Dave quit at level 2").

**Top bar.** Timer, page and level indicator, rage meter, mute, and an "I give up" button that always works.

## End screen

The results card shows:
- title
- time
- level reached
- failed clicks
- rage %
- score

Titles:
- **Zen Monk:** you beat level 12 by waiting it out
- **Mildly Annoyed:** rage below 25%
- **Keyboard Warrior:** rage below 50%
- **Table Flipper:** rage below 80%
- **Monitor Defenestrator:** rage 80% or above

Score is `(level reached x 100) + seconds survived - rage %`.

**Shame leaderboard.**
- Top 5 scores, saved in the browser's localStorage under the key `rq_lb`.
- All storage calls are wrapped in try/catch, so the game still works with storage disabled.

**Secret reward.** The card includes a "Claim your Calm Certificate" button. It opens the classic rickroll video on YouTube in a new tab. The tab only opens when the user clicks, because browsers block automatic new tabs.

## Safety and fair play

- A flashing-lights and shaking warning is on the intro card.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` is respected (shake, ticker and scroll reveals are turned off).
- "I give up" works at all times.
- No back-button hijacking, no `beforeunload` trap, no tracking, no network requests apart from Google Fonts. The rickroll opens only on click.
- Every level is beatable, and the Mercy button guarantees progress.

## Code layout (inside `ragequit.html`)

| Section | Responsibility |
|---------|----------------|
| `S` | Global state: rage, level, fails, start time, mute, live flag |
| `beep` / `snd` | WebAudio sound effects |
| `L` / `say()` | Narrator lines and speech bubble |
| `rage()` | Meter fill, background color, tilt, scanlines, shake |
| `fail()` / `mercy()` | Fail penalties, hints, the skip button |
| `LV` | Level registry: array of `{name, hint, init(area, win)}` |
| `build(p)` | Renders page p (0 or 1) with locked level cards |
| `activate(i)` | Unlocks level i, scrolls to it, runs `init`, handles win |
| `end2()` | Builds the results card, saves the leaderboard |
| `board()` | Renders the leaderboard |

## Add a new level

1. Add an object to the `LV` array: `{name:"My Level", hint:"...", init(a, win){ ... return ()=>{ /* cleanup */ } }}`.
2. In `init`, build your HTML inside the container `a`.
3. Call `win()` when the player beats it and `fail(n)` for a tracked fail (n is the rage added).
4. Return a cleanup function that clears your intervals and listeners.
5. Update the level counts: the page split in `build()` (6 per page) and the `S.l>=12` / `S.l==6` checks in `activate()`.

## Known limitations

- Not built yet: inverted-mouse Opposite Day, canvas share card, looping background music, mid-hit label swaps in Whack-a-Mole.
- Leftover code from earlier versions remains (an unused start screen, an unused results screen and the old `load0` function). It doesn't affect gameplay and can be deleted.
- Not tested on real phones. Hover-based levels (1, 6, 7) behave differently on touch.
- The game has not been run in a browser by its author, so expect some rough edges.

